---
import katex from 'katex';
// Render each label's math to HTML at build time (robust — no client-side dependency).
const m = (tex) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: false });

// The argument in five links. Each link carries its honest status colour.
const links = [
  { n: '1 · Finite information (P4-MICRO)',
    t: `The postulate is just that a region ${m('R')} has a <em>finite</em> information capacity — a finite number ${m('N_R')} of distinguishable microstates (a UV-finite record structure). This alone gives only ${m('S_{\\mathrm{vN}}(\\rho_R) \\le \\log N_R')}; it does <strong>not</strong> by itself fix whether ${m('\\log N_R')} scales with <em>area</em> or volume. That the bound takes the <strong>holographic area form</strong> (${m('\\log N_R \\le A/4\\ell_P^2')}) is <em>derived</em> in a <em>conditional</em> Sakharov / induced-gravity bridge (the conical-deficit heat-kernel calculation, assuming local relativistic QFT on a smooth background with a covariant cutoff identified with the finiteness) — <strong>not</strong> from finiteness alone. There the area law emerges and the <strong>1/4</strong> is the universal ratio between the conical replica-entropy coefficient and the induced Einstein–Hilbert coefficient — two quantities sharing one UV coefficient; the <em>ratio</em> is machine-checked, the <em>value</em> of ${m('G')} is not (plus, for the full action, ${m('\\Lambda')} and higher-curvature terms are carried). It is the kinematic engine of gravity: the area floor and (free field) the Einstein equations follow from it. It is <em>not</em> the single-outcome mechanism — λ uses only this <em>finiteness</em>, and there is no “capacity forbids superpositions” postulate.`,
    cls: 's-postulate', tag: 'Postulate (P4-MICRO / FQ)' },
  { n: '2 · Regional cost',
    t: `The local algebra of a region is <em>Type III₁</em> (no trace); CPW's gravitational crossed product dresses it into a <em>Type II</em> algebra with a trace, on which the regional cost — Araki relative entropy ${m('\\chi_R')} — is finite. This Type II is the <em>entropy device</em>, not λ's home: λ stays <em>Type-III-native</em> (the earlier "dress λ into Type II for atoms" idea is retired — Type II has no minimal projections either).`,
    cls: 's-verified', tag: 'Calculus verified' },
  { n: '3 · Decoherence',
    t: `Standard unitary decoherence makes the macroscopic records non-interfering and redundantly objective; <em>einselection</em> (the interaction Hamiltonian) fixes <em>which</em> record framework — machine-checked as a no-go trilogy (capacity, symmetry, and the state Φ each provably fail to select it; the interaction does). But this removes interference and fixes the framework — it does not make one record actual.`,
    cls: 's-verified', tag: 'Standard QM' },
  { n: '4 · Single record',
    t: `A non-dynamical selector λ makes exactly one record actual; Φ stays exactly unitary. λ is a finite <em>index</em> over those records — its information bounded by the holographic stage of link 1 (${m('H(\\lambda)\\le H(R)\\le Q_R')}), the one place holography meets the selector, and only this finite-index/exact-Born form survives. Its <em>selection schema</em> is machine-checked (finite → free field): the Takesaki criterion fixes which record context, the dephasing map is <em>modular-invariant</em> (a consistency fact — modular flow ≠ physical time), and an explicit inverse-CDF constructor picks <em>exactly one</em> record per actuality seed with seed-measure = Born weight. It's a verified schema, not a law: the single outcome is λ's, and the irreducible primitive is just <em>which seed is actual</em>.`,
    cls: 's-conditional', tag: 'Selection schema (λ) · verified' },
  { n: '5 · Born',
    t: `Probabilities ${m('|c_k|^2')} from typicality — reduced (machine-checked) to a single typicality postulate (the relativistic analogue of quantum equilibrium), with a no-go that some such premise is unavoidable; not yet derived.`,
    cls: 's-open', tag: 'Open · reduced' },
];
---
<div class="chain">
  {links.map((l) => (
    <div class="link">
      <div class="n">{l.n}</div>
      <div class="t" set:html={l.t}></div>
      <span class={`pill ${l.cls}`}><span class="dot"></span>{l.tag}</span>
    </div>
  ))}
</div>
